Adding 10 to every value in a population increases μ by 10 and leaves σ unchanged."}]}]},"e":"True. Every deviation (x + 10) − (μ + 10) equals x − μ, so the spread does not change.","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Standard deviation","st":"shift invariance","cfg":{"question_number":12,"paper_question_key":12,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":13,"t":"true_false","p":"On a cumulative frequency curve for 120 items, $Q_3$ is read at cumulative frequency 90.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"On a cumulative frequency curve for 120 items, "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"Q_3"}},{"type":"text","text":" is read at cumulative frequency 90."}]}]},"e":"True, because $Q_3$ lies at $\\frac{3}{4}N=\\frac{3}{4}(120)=90$.","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Cumulative frequency","st":"quartile position","cfg":{"question_number":13,"paper_question_key":13,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":14,"t":"true_false","p":"Increasing only the largest value in an ordered set ($N \\ge 5$) always increases both range and IQR.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Increasing only the largest value in an ordered set ("},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"N \\ge 5"}},{"type":"text","text":") always increases both range and IQR."}]}]},"e":"False. The range increases, but for N ≥ 5 the quartiles do not use the max