stimated mean from a grouped table","cfg":{"question_number":19,"paper_question_key":19,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","numeric_answer":16.5,"tolerance":0,"source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":20,"t":"numeric","p":"Six basketball players have mean height 174 cm. A seventh player joins and the new mean is 176 cm. Find the seventh player''s height.  [cm]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Six basketball players have mean height 174 cm. A seventh player joins and the new mean is 176 cm. Find the seventh player''s height.  [cm]"}]}]},"e":"Total of six is 6 × 174 = 1044 cm; total of seven is 7 × 176 = 1232 cm; the seventh player is 1232 − 1044 = 188 cm.","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Combined mean","st":"finding a seventh value","cfg":{"question_number":20,"paper_question_key":20,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","numeric_answer":188,"tolerance":0,"source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":21,"t":"single_choice","p":"The cumulative frequency curve shows lifetimes (h) of 80 cells. Estimate the median and IQR.\n\n[Figure: Lifetimes of 80 cells]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The cumulative frequency curve shows lifetimes (h) of 80 cells. Estimate the median and IQR."}]},{"type":"assessDiagramBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"kind":"cumulative_curve","title":"Lifetimes of 80 cells","xLabel":"Lifetime (h)","yLabel":"Cum. freq.","xMin":0,"xMax":50,"yMin":0,"yMax":80,"points":[[0,0],[10,3],[15,8],[20,20],[25,30],[30,40],[35,60],[40,70],[45,76],[50,80]],"joinPoints":true,"version":1}}}]},"e":"At CF 40 the median is 30 h; at CF 20, Q1 = 20 h and at CF 60, Q3 = 35 h. IQR = 35 − 20 = 15 h.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Cumulative frequency","st":"reading median and quartiles","cfg":{"question_number":21,"paper_question_key":21,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":22,"t":"single_choice","p":"The unequal-width histogram shows pebble masses using frequency density. How many pebbles were measured in total?\n\n[Figure: Pebble masses]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The unequal-width histogram shows pebble masses using frequency density. How many pebbles were measured in total?"}]},{"type":"assessDiagramBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"kind":"histogram","title":"Pebble masses","xLabel":"Mass, m (g)","yLabel":"Freq. density","xMin":0,"xMax":40,"yMin":0,"yMax":4,"bars":[{"from":0,"to":10,"height":1.5},{"from":10,"to":20,"height":3.5},{"from":20,"to":40,"height":2.0}],"version":1}}}]},"e":"Frequency = width × frequency density: 10(1.5) + 10(3.5) + 20(2.0) = 15 + 35 + 40 = 90.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Histograms","st":"total frequency from frequency density","cfg":{"question_number":22,"paper_question_key":22,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":23,"t":"single_choice","p":"Grouped classes $0 \\le x < 4$ $(f = 5)$, $4 \\le x < 8$ $(f = k)$ and $8 \\le x < 12$ $(f = 7)$ yield an estimated mean of 6.4. Find $k$.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Grouped classes "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"0 \\le x < 4"}},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(f = 5)"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"4 \\le x < 8"}},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(f = k)"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"8 \\le x < 12"}},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(f = 7)"}},{"type":"text","text":" yield an estimated mean of 6.4. Find "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"k"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},"e":"$\\frac{5(2)+6k+7(10)}{12+k}=6.4$ gives $80+6k=76.8+6.4k$, so $0.4k=3.2$ and $k=8$.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Estimated mean","st":"finding a missing frequency","cfg":{"question_number":23,"paper_question_key":23,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":24,"t":"single_choice","p":"Sixty commute times (min) have Min = 8, Q1 = 22, Med = 29, Q3 = 34, Max = 56. Using upper outlier fence $Q_3 + 1.5 \\times IQR$, which statement is true?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sixty commute times (min) have Min = 8, Q1 = 22, Med = 29, Q3 = 34, Max = 56. Using upper outlier fence "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"Q_3 + 1.5 \\times IQR"}},{"type":"text","text":", which statement is true?"}]}]},"e":"IQR = 34 − 22 = 12, so the upper fence is 34 + 1.5(12) = 52. Since 56 > 52, a high outlier is present.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Outlier fences","st":"upper fence","cfg":{"question_number":24,"paper_question_key":24,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":25,"t":"single_choice","p":"A population of 25 readings has $\\mu = 12.0$ and $\\sigma = 2.5$. Every value $x$ is transformed to $y = 2x - 5$. What are the new $\\mu_y$ and $\\sigma_y$?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A population of 25 readings has "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\mu = 12.0"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\sigma = 2.5"}},{"type":"text","text":". Every value "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"x"}},{"type":"text","text":" is transformed to "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"y = 2x - 5"}},{"type":"text","text":". What are the new "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\mu_y"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\sigma_y"}},{"type":"text","text":"?"}]}]},"e":"$\\mu_y=2(12.0)-5=19.0$ and $\\sigma_y=|2|(2.5)=5.0$; the −5 shift does not affect spread.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Linear transformation","st":"mean and standard deviation under coding","cfg":{"question_number":25,"paper_question_key":25,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":26,"t":"open_ended","p":"Data classification & graph selection\nClassify each variable as qualitative, quantitative discrete, or quantitative continuous, and name a suitable chart (bar graph or histogram) for (b) and (c):\n(a) Primary language spoken at home (Fon, French, English, Yoruba). [1]\n(b) Number of library books borrowed per student in October. [1]\n(c) Time (in seconds) taken by Grade 9 sprinters to run 400 m. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Data classification & graph selection","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Classify each variable as qualitative, quantitative discrete, or quantitative continuous, and name a suitable chart (bar graph or histogram) for (b) and (c):"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Primary language spoken at home (Fon, French, English, Yoruba). [1]\n(b) Number of library books borrowed per student in October. [1]\n(c) Time (in seconds) taken by Grade 9 sprinters to run 400 m. [1]"}]}]},"e":"(a) Qualitative (categorical). (b) Quantitative discrete; a bar graph with separated bars for the whole-number counts (a discrete frequency histogram is also acceptable if stated). (c) Quantitative continuous; a histogram with adjacent bars over continuous time intervals.","m":3,"df":"easy","tp":"Types of data & graph selection","st":"classifying variables and choosing a chart","cfg":{"question_number":26,"paper_question_key":26,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"basic","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":1,"focus":"Qualitative language data"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"Discrete count and bar graph"},{"label":"(c)","marks":1,"focus":"Continuous time and histogram"}],"response_guidance":"Both the classification and an appropriate chart are required in (b) and (c).","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math"],"paper_title":"Data classification & graph selection","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":27,"t":"open_ended","p":"Central tendency & dispersion\nGoals scored per match by a school team in 12 matches: 2, 0, 3, 1, 2, 4, 2, 1, 0, 3, 2, 4.\n(a) Find the mode, median, and range of goals scored. [2]\n(b) Calculate the exact mean number of goals per match. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Central tendency & dispersion","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Goals scored per match by a school team in 12 matches: 2, 0, 3, 1, 2, 4, 2, 1, 0, 3, 2, 4."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Find the mode, median, and range of goals scored. [2]\n(b) Calculate the exact mean number of goals per match. [1]"}]}]},"e":"Ordered: 0, 0, 1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4. Mode = 2; median = (2 + 2)/2 = 2; range = 4 − 0 = 4. (b) Σx = 24, so mean = 24/12 = 2 goals.","m":3,"df":"easy","tp":"Central tendency & dispersion","st":"mode, median, range and mean","cfg":{"question_number":27,"paper_question_key":27,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"basic","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Mode, median and range"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"Exact mean"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math"],"paper_title":"Central tendency & dispersion","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":28,"t":"open_ended","p":"Grouped discrete-limit distribution\nMasses (to the nearest gram) of 50 harvested cocoa pods are given in the table.\n\n[Figure: table]\n(a) State the lower & upper class boundaries of 320–339, the modal class, and the class containing the median. [2]\n(b) Using exact mid-interval values, calculate an estimate of the mean mass. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Grouped discrete-limit distribution","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Masses (to the nearest gram) of 50 harvested cocoa pods are given in the table."}]},{"type":"assessDiagramBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"kind":"table","title":"","columns":["Mass (g)","300–319","320–339","340–359","360–379","380–399"],"rows":[["Freq. (f)","6","14","18","8","4"]],"version":1}}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the lower & upper class boundaries of 320–339, the modal class, and the class containing the median. [2]\n(b) Using exact mid-interval values, calculate an estimate of the mean mass. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) 319.5 g and 339.5 g; modal and median class are both 340–359 g (cumulative frequencies 6, 20, 38, 46, 50). (b) Midpoints 309.5, 329.5, 349.5, 369.5, 389.5 give Σfx = 17 275, so the estimated mean is 345.5 g.","m":4,"df":"medium","tp":"Grouped discrete-limit distribution","st":"boundaries, modal/median class and estimated mean","cfg":{"question_number":28,"paper_question_key":28,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Boundaries and classes"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Estimated mean"}],"response_guidance":"Use exact mid-interval values; a single midpoint instead of the class interval loses the second mark in (a).","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table"],"paper_title":"Grouped discrete-limit distribution","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":29,"t":"open_ended","p":"Population standard deviation\nA robotics sensor logs a complete population of $N = 6$ voltages (V): 9, 11, 12, 12, 14, 14.\n(a) Calculate the population mean μ and each squared deviation $(x - \\mu)^2$. [2]\n(b) Find the population standard deviation σ in exact surd form and to 2 d.p. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Population standard deviation","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A robotics sensor logs a complete population of "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"N = 6"}},{"type":"text","text":" voltages (V): 9, 11, 12, 12, 14, 14."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the population mean μ and each squared deviation "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(x - \\mu)^2"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]\n(b) Find the population standard deviation σ in exact surd form and to 2 d.p. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) $\\mu=72/6=12$ V; squared deviations are 9, 1, 0, 0, 4, 4 (sum 18). (b) $\\sigma=\\sqrt{18/6}=\\sqrt{3}\\approx1.73$ V.","m":4,"df":"medium","tp":"Population standard deviation","st":"squared deviations and exact surd","cfg":{"question_number":29,"paper_question_key":29,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Mean and squared deviations"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Exact and decimal σ"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math"],"paper_title":"Population standard deviation","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":30,"t":"open_ended","p":"Cumulative frequency curve\nThe ogive shows the masses (g) of 60 harvested mangoes.\n\n[Figure: Masses of 60 mangoes]\n(a) Estimate Q2, Q1, Q3 and the IQR. [2]\n(b) Mangoes above 350 g are Premium. Estimate how many qualify and their percentage. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cumulative frequency curve","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The ogive shows the masses (g) of 60 harvested mangoes."}]},{"type":"assessDiagramBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"kind":"cumulative_curve","title":"Masses of 60 mangoes","xLabel":"Mass (g)","yLabel":"Cum. freq.","xMin":150,"xMax":400,"yMin":0,"yMax":60,"points":[[150,0],[200,6],[225,15],[250,21],[275,30],[300,39],[325,45],[350,54],[400,60]],"joinPoints":true,"version":1}}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Estimate Q2, Q1, Q3 and the IQR. [2]\n(b) Mangoes above 350 g are Premium. Estimate how many qualify and their percentage. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) At CF 30, Q2 = 275 g; at CF 15, Q1 = 225 g; at CF 45, Q3 = 325 g; IQR = 100 g. (b) At 350 g, CF = 54, so 60 − 54 = 6 mangoes are Premium, which is 10%.","m":4,"df":"medium","tp":"Cumulative frequency curve","st":"quartiles and a contextual count","cfg":{"question_number":30,"paper_question_key":30,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Q2, Q1, Q3 and IQR"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Premium count and percentage"}],"response_guidance":"Allow ±3 g reading tolerance on each quartile and follow through the IQR.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","graph"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","graph"],"paper_title":"Cumulative frequency curve","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":31,"t":"open_ended","p":"Unequal widths & frequency density\nClinic waiting times $t$ (min) for 64 patients:\n\n[Figure: table]\n(a) Calculate the class width and frequency density for all four classes. [2]\n(b) If 1 cm on the vertical axis represents 0.5 units of frequency density, state the four bar heights (cm) and estimate how many patients waited 10 ≤ t < 25 min. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unequal widths & frequency density","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Clinic waiting times "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"t"}},{"type":"text","text":" (min) for 64 patients:"}]},{"type":"assessDiagramBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"kind":"table","title":"","columns":["Time t (min)","0 ≤ t < 5","5 ≤ t < 15","15 ≤ t < 30","30 ≤ t < 50"],"rows":[["Freq. (f)","10","28","18","8"]],"version":1}}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the class width and frequency density for all four classes. [2]\n(b) If 1 cm on the vertical axis represents 0.5 units of frequency density, state the four bar heights (cm) and estimate how many patients waited 10 ≤ t < 25 min. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) Widths 5, 10, 15, 20; frequency densities 2.0, 2.8, 1.2, 0.4. (b) Heights are 4.0, 5.6, 2.4, 0.8 cm. Patients in 10 ≤ t < 25: 5(2.8) + 10(1.2) = 14 + 12 = 26.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"Unequal widths & frequency density","st":"frequency density and histogram scaling","cfg":{"question_number":31,"paper_question_key":31,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Widths and frequency densities"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Bar heights and a class count"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math","table","graph"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table","graph"],"paper_title":"Unequal widths & frequency density","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":32,"t":"open_ended","p":"Outlier sensitivity pattern\nSeven daily rainfalls (mm) are 12, 14, 15, 15, 16, 18, 22. On Day 8, a storm records $x$ mm $(x \\ge 22)$.\n(a) Find the 7-day mean, median & IQR; then recalculate all three for 8 days when $x = 64$ mm. [2]\n(b) Express the 8-day mean in terms of $x$, and explain why the 8-day median and IQR stay constant for all $x \\ge 22$. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Outlier sensitivity pattern","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Seven daily rainfalls (mm) are 12, 14, 15, 15, 16, 18, 22. On Day 8, a storm records "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"x"}},{"type":"text","text":" mm "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(x \\ge 22)"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Find the 7-day mean, median & IQR; then recalculate all three for 8 days when "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"x = 64"}},{"type":"text","text":" mm. [2]\n(b) Express the 8-day mean in terms of "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"x"}},{"type":"text","text":", and explain why the 8-day median and IQR stay constant for all "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"x \\ge 22"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) 7-day mean 16 mm, median 15 mm, Q1 14, Q3 18, IQR 4 mm. With x = 64: mean 22 mm, median 15.5 mm, Q1 14.5, Q3 20, IQR 5.5 mm. (b) Mean = (112 + x)/8 = 14 + x/8. For every x ≥ 22 the ordered list keeps x last, so positions 4/5 remain 15 and 16 and positions 2/3 and 6/7 remain 14/15 and 18/22; median and IQR are therefore fixed.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"Outlier sensitivity pattern","st":"mean, median and IQR under an extreme value","cfg":{"question_number":32,"paper_question_key":32,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Statistics with and without x"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Formula and invariance proof"}],"response_guidance":"The invariance mark requires an order-position argument, not just “outliers do not affect the median”.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table"],"paper_title":"Outlier sensitivity pattern","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":33,"t":"open_ended","p":"Scaling & shifting populations\nThree populations of size $N = 4$ are $P_1 = \\{2, 4, 6, 8\\}$, $P_2 = \\{12, 14, 16, 18\\}$ $(y = x + 10)$ and $P_3 = \\{6, 12, 18, 24\\}$ $(z = 3x)$.\n(a) Given $\\mu_1 = 5$ and $\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{5}$, find $(\\mu_2, \\sigma_2)$ and $(\\mu_3, \\sigma_3)$ in exact surd form. [2]\n(b) State general rules for $\\mu_w$ and $\\sigma_w$ when $w = ax + b$ $(a > 0)$, and algebraically prove $\\sigma_w = a\\sigma_x$. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Scaling & shifting populations","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Three populations of size "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"N = 4"}},{"type":"text","text":" are "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"P_1 = \\{2, 4, 6, 8\\}"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"P_2 = \\{12, 14, 16, 18\\}"}},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(y = x + 10)"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"P_3 = \\{6, 12, 18, 24\\}"}},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(z = 3x)"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Given "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\mu_1 = 5"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{5}"}},{"type":"text","text":", find "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(\\mu_2, \\sigma_2)"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(\\mu_3, \\sigma_3)"}},{"type":"text","text":" in exact surd form. [2]\n(b) State general rules for "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\mu_w"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\sigma_w"}},{"type":"text","text":" when "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"w = ax + b"}},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(a > 0)"}},{"type":"text","text":", and algebraically prove "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\sigma_w = a\\sigma_x"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) $\\mu_2=15$, $\\sigma_2=\\sqrt{5}$; $\\mu_3=15$, $\\sigma_3=\\sqrt{45}=3\\sqrt{5}$. (b) $\\mu_w=a\\mu_x+b$ and $\\sigma_w=a\\sigma_x$; since $w-\\mu_w=a(x-\\mu_x)$, $\\sigma_w=\\sqrt{a^2\\Sigma(x-\\mu_x)^2/N}=a\\sigma_x$.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"Scaling & shifting populations","st":"effect of affine transformations on mean and σ","cfg":{"question_number":33,"paper_question_key":33,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Mean and σ for both transformed populations"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"General rules and proof"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math"],"paper_title":"Scaling & shifting populations","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":34,"t":"open_ended","p":"Class-width granularity & estimated mean\nTen raw marks (exact mean 20.5) are: 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 26, 28, 34, 36.\n(a) Group into two wide classes $(10 \\le x < 25, 25 \\le x < 40)$ to find estimated mean $m_A$, then into three narrower classes $(10 \\le x < 20, 20 \\le x < 30, 30 \\le x < 40)$ to find $m_B$. [2]\n(b) Explain why $m_A$ overestimates 20.5 sharply and generalise how class width affects midpoint-estimate accuracy. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Class-width granularity & estimated mean","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ten raw marks (exact mean 20.5) are: 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 26, 28, 34, 36."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Group into two wide classes "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(10 \\le x < 25, 25 \\le x < 40)"}},{"type":"text","text":" to find estimated mean "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"m_A"}},{"type":"text","text":", then into three narrower classes "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(10 \\le x < 20, 20 \\le x < 30, 30 \\le x < 40)"}},{"type":"text","text":" to find "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"m_B"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]\n(b) Explain why "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"m_A"}},{"type":"text","text":" overestimates 20.5 sharply and generalise how class width affects midpoint-estimate accuracy. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) Wide: 6 at mid 17.5 and 4 at mid 32.5, so mA = 23.5. Narrow: 6 at 15, 2 at 25, 2 at 35, so mB = 21.0. (b) The six low values average 13.5 but are represented by 17.5, adding 4 to each; narrower classes reduce the maximum distance from raw values to midpoints and therefore reduce grouping bias.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"Class-width granularity","st":"estimated mean and grouping bias","cfg":{"question_number":34,"paper_question_key":34,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Both estimated means"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Reason and generalisation"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table"],"paper_title":"Class-width granularity & estimated mean","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":35,"t":"open_ended","p":"Quartile spacing & skewness\nThree score sets share $Q_2 = 60$: Set X $(Q_1 = 42, Q_3 = 78)$; Set Y $(Q_1 = 54, Q_3 = 82)$; Set Z $(Q_1 = 30, Q_3 = 66)$.\n(a) Compare $Q_2 - Q_1$ with $Q_3 - Q_2$ to classify X, Y and Z as symmetric, positively skewed or negatively skewed. [2]\n(b) Write an inequality in $Q_1, Q_2, Q_3$ for positive skew and describe how its ogive slope changes from $Q_1$ to $Q_3$. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Quartile spacing & skewness","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Three score sets share "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"Q_2 = 60"}},{"type":"text","text":": Set X "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(Q_1 = 42, Q_3 = 78)"}},{"type":"text","text":"; Set Y "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(Q_1 = 54, Q_3 = 82)"}},{"type":"text","text":"; Set Z "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(Q_1 = 30, Q_3 = 66)"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Compare "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"Q_2 - Q_1"}},{"type":"text","text":" with "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"Q_3 - Q_2"}},{"type":"text","text":" to classify X, Y and Z as symmetric, positively skewed or negatively skewed. [2]\n(b) Write an inequality in "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"Q_1, Q_2, Q_3"}},{"type":"text","text":" for positive skew and describe how its ogive slope changes from "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"Q_1"}},{"type":"text","text":" to "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"Q_3"}},{"type":"text","text":". [1]"}]}]},"e":"(a) X: 18 and 18 → symmetric. Y: 6 < 22 → positively skewed. Z: 30 > 6 → negatively skewed. (b) Positive skew: Q3 − Q2 > Q2 − Q1 (equivalently Q1 + Q3 > 2Q2); the ogive is steep from Q1 to Q2 and flatter from Q2 to Q3.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"Quartile spacing & skewness","st":"classifying skew from quartiles","cfg":{"question_number":35,"paper_question_key":35,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Compare quartile spacings"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"Inequality and ogive shape"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math"],"paper_title":"Quartile spacing & skewness","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":36,"t":"open_ended","p":"Back-to-back stem-and-leaf diagram\nPulse rates (bpm) of 10 swimmers:\nRest: 68, 72, 64, 75, 81, 68, 70, 77, 63, 72  |  Post-warm-up: 84, 92, 78, 88, 95, 84, 90, 102, 86, 91.\n(a) Construct an ordered back-to-back stem-and-leaf diagram (stems 6 to 10) with a dual key. [3]\n(b) Compare the two distributions in context using their medians and ranges. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Back-to-back stem-and-leaf diagram","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Pulse rates (bpm) of 10 swimmers:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rest: 68, 72, 64, 75, 81, 68, 70, 77, 63, 72  |  Post-warm-up: 84, 92, 78, 88, 95, 84, 90, 102, 86, 91."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Construct an ordered back-to-back stem-and-leaf diagram (stems 6 to 10) with a dual key. [3]\n(b) Compare the two distributions in context using their medians and ranges. [1]"}]}]},"e":"(a) Rest leaves (outward, right-to-left): stem 6: 8, 8, 4, 3; stem 7: 5, 2, 2, 0; stem 8: 1. Post leaves: stem 6: 7; stem 7: 8; stem 8: 4, 4, 6, 8; stem 9: 0, 1, 2, 5; stem 10: 2. Dual key, e.g. 4 | 6 | 8 means 64 bpm (rest) and 68 bpm (post). (b) Rest median 71 bpm and range 18 bpm; post median 89 bpm and range 24 bpm: post rates are substantially higher and slightly more spread out.","m":4,"df":"medium","tp":"Back-to-back stem-and-leaf","st":"constructing and comparing distributions","cfg":{"question_number":36,"paper_question_key":36,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":3,"focus":"Complete ordered diagram and dual key"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"Comparison using median and range"}],"response_guidance":"Left-side leaves must increase outward from the stem; all 20 leaves and the units of the dual key are required.","response_tools":["stem_leaf","rich_text"],"tool_labels":["stem_leaf","rich_text"],"paper_title":"Back-to-back stem-and-leaf diagram","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":37,"t":"open_ended","p":"Constructing a continuous grouped table\nHeights $h$ (cm) of 20 seedlings: 6.4, 11.2, 8.5, 14.0, 17.8, 9.1, 12.6, 7.3, 15.5, 10.0, 13.4, 18.2, 11.9, 5.8, 14.7, 9.9, 16.1, 12.0, 8.0, 13.8.\n(a) Construct a grouped table with classes $5 \\le h < 10$, $10 \\le h < 15$, $15 \\le h < 20$, midpoints and frequencies. [2]\n(b) Explain why writing class labels “5–10, 10–15, 15–20” is ambiguous for $h = 10.0$ cm. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Constructing a continuous grouped table","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Heights "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"h"}},{"type":"text","text":" (cm) of 20 seedlings: 6.4, 11.2, 8.5, 14.0, 17.8, 9.1, 12.6, 7.3, 15.5, 10.0, 13.4, 18.2, 11.9, 5.8, 14.7, 9.9, 16.1, 12.0, 8.0, 13.8."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Construct a grouped table with classes "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"5 \\le h < 10"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"10 \\le h < 15"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"15 \\le h < 20"}},{"type":"text","text":", midpoints and frequencies. [2]\n(b) Explain why writing class labels “5–10, 10–15, 15–20” is ambiguous for "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"h = 10.0"}},{"type":"text","text":" cm. [1]"}]}]},"e":"(a) 5 ≤ h < 10: midpoint 7.5, f = 7; 10 ≤ h < 15: midpoint 12.5, f = 9; 15 ≤ h < 20: midpoint 17.5, f = 4; total 20. (b) The labels share the endpoint 10, so it is unclear whether 10.0 belongs to the first or second class; the strict inequalities place it uniquely in the second class.","m":3,"df":"medium","tp":"Constructing a grouped table","st":"continuous classes and ambiguity","cfg":{"question_number":37,"paper_question_key":37,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Grouped table and frequencies"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"Boundary ambiguity"}],"response_tools":["data_table","rich_text","math"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table"],"paper_title":"Constructing a continuous grouped table","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d","response_requirement":"data_table"}},{"n":38,"t":"open_ended","p":"Critiquing & constructing a histogram\nTobi plots continuous broadband speeds $0 \\le s < 10$ $(f = 6)$, $10 \\le s < 20$ $(f = 14)$, $20 \\le s < 40$ $(f = 12)$ as shown.\n\n[Figure: Tobi''s broadband-speed chart]\n(a) Identify two statistical/graphical errors in Tobi''s chart. [2]\n(b) Draw a correct, fully labelled frequency-density histogram in your script. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Critiquing & constructing a histogram","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tobi plots continuous broadband speeds "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"0 \\le s < 10"}},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(f = 6)"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"10 \\le s < 20"}},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(f = 14)"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"20 \\le s < 40"}},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(f = 12)"}},{"type":"text","text":" as shown."}]},{"type":"statsBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"version":1,"chart":"bar_chart","title":"Tobi''s broadband-speed chart","xLabel":"Speed (Mbps)","yLabel":"Freq.","values":[],"categories":[{"id":"c0","label":"0–10","value":6},{"id":"c1","label":"10–20","value":14},{"id":"c2","label":"20–40","value":12}],"binWidth":null,"showSummary":false,"dataMode":"raw"}}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Identify two statistical/graphical errors in Tobi''s chart. [2]\n(b) Draw a correct, fully labelled frequency-density histogram in your script. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) Any two of: continuous data should have adjacent bars (no gaps); the vertical axis plots frequency instead of frequency density; the 20–40 bar is drawn the same width as the 10-wide bars although its class is twice as wide. (b) Frequency densities are 0.6, 1.4 and 0.6; draw adjacent bars, the third twice as wide, with both axes labelled.","m":4,"df":"medium","tp":"Critiquing a histogram","st":"frequency density and equal bar widths","cfg":{"question_number":38,"paper_question_key":38,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Two distinct errors"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Correct histogram"}],"response_tools":["statistics","graph","rich_text","math"],"tool_labels":["graph","rich_text","math"],"paper_title":"Critiquing & constructing a histogram","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d","response_requirement":"statistics","response_stats":{"chart":"histogram"}}},{"n":39,"t":"open_ended","p":"Comparing box-and-whisker diagrams\nScience scores (%) for Class 9A (32 students) and Class 9B (32 students) are shown.\n\n[Figure: Science scores (%) for Classes 9A and 9B]\n(a) State the five-number summary of 9A and calculate the IQR of both classes. [2]\n(b) Write two structured comparisons (average & consistency) citing numerical values. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Comparing box-and-whisker diagrams","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Science scores (%) for Class 9A (32 students) and Class 9B (32 students) are shown."}]},{"type":"assessDiagramBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"kind":"box_plot","title":"Science scores (%) for Classes 9A and 9B","xLabel":"Score (%)","xMin":20,"xMax":90,"boxPlots":[{"label":"9A","min":35,"q1":52,"median":64,"q3":74,"max":88},{"label":"9B","min":22,"q1":44,"median":64,"q3":80,"max":88}],"version":1}}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the five-number summary of 9A and calculate the IQR of both classes. [2]\n(b) Write two structured comparisons (average & consistency) citing numerical values. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) 9A: Min 35, Q1 52, median 64, Q3 74, Max 88, IQR 22. 9B: Q1 44, Q3 80, IQR 36. (b) Both classes have the same median (64%), so typical performance is identical, but 9A is much more consistent: IQR 22% versus 36% and range 53% versus 66%.","m":4,"df":"medium","tp":"Comparing box-and-whisker diagrams","st":"five-number summary and contextual comparison","cfg":{"question_number":39,"paper_question_key":39,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Summary and both IQRs"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Average and consistency comparison"}],"response_guidance":"Bare numerical listings without contextual interpretation earn at most 1 of 2 in (b).","response_tools":["graph","rich_text","math"],"tool_labels":["graph","rich_text","math"],"paper_title":"Comparing box-and-whisker diagrams","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":40,"t":"open_ended","p":"Precision in statistical language\n(a) From modal class $20 \\le t < 30$ $(f = 18)$, Adama claims: “The mode is 25.” Explain why this is invalid. [1]\n(b) State the formula for population σ, distinguish σ from sample s, and state when σ applies. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Precision in statistical language","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) From modal class "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"20 \\le t < 30"}},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(f = 18)"}},{"type":"text","text":", Adama claims: “The mode is 25.” Explain why this is invalid. [1]\n(b) State the formula for population σ, distinguish σ from sample s, and state when σ applies. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) 25 is only the class midpoint; the individual raw values are unknown, so no observation need equal 25. (b) $\\sigma=\\sqrt{\\Sigma(x-\\mu)^2/N}$ (or the frequency form). σ divides by N and describes a complete population; sample s divides by n − 1 (Bessel''s correction) when the data are a subset used to estimate population spread.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"Precision in statistical language","st":"modal class vs mode and population σ","cfg":{"question_number":40,"paper_question_key":40,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":1,"focus":"Modal class vs mode"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"σ formula and distinction"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math"],"paper_title":"Precision in statistical language","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":41,"t":"open_ended","p":"Solar mini-grid demand\nDaily electricity use $E$ (kWh) across 40 Abomey-Calavi homes:\n\n[Figure: table]\n(a) Calculate the estimated mean daily usage per home. [2]\n(b) The mini-grid supplies 315 kWh/day. Check if estimated total demand is met and explain why actual demand could still exceed 315 kWh. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Solar mini-grid demand","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Daily electricity use "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"E"}},{"type":"text","text":" (kWh) across 40 Abomey-Calavi homes:"}]},{"type":"assessDiagramBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"kind":"table","title":"","columns":["Usage E (kWh)","0 ≤ E < 4","4 ≤ E < 8","8 ≤ E < 12","12 ≤ E < 16"],"rows":[["Homes (f)","8","15","11","6"]],"version":1}}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the estimated mean daily usage per home. [2]\n(b) The mini-grid supplies 315 kWh/day. Check if estimated total demand is met and explain why actual demand could still exceed 315 kWh. [1]"}]}]},"e":"(a) Midpoints 2, 6, 10, 14 give ΣfE = 300 and mean 7.5 kWh. (b) Estimated demand is 40 × 7.5 = 300 kWh ≤ 315, but midpoint grouping can understate demand: if many homes use near the upper end of each class, actual demand can exceed 315 kWh.","m":3,"df":"medium","tp":"Solar mini-grid demand","st":"estimated mean and capacity judgement","cfg":{"question_number":41,"paper_question_key":41,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Estimated mean"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"Capacity check and uncertainty"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table"],"paper_title":"Solar mini-grid demand","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":42,"t":"open_ended","p":"Water-sachet quality control\nAn entire batch population of $N = 10$ sachets has volumes:\n\n[Figure: table]\n(a) Calculate the population mean μ and population standard deviation σ (to 2 d.p.). [2]\n(b) Batch approval requires $499.0 \\le \\mu \\le 501.0$ mL and $\\sigma \\le 2.00$ mL. Justify whether the batch passes. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Water-sachet quality control","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"An entire batch population of "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"N = 10"}},{"type":"text","text":" sachets has volumes:"}]},{"type":"assessDiagramBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"kind":"table","title":"","columns":["Volume x (mL)","496","498","500","502","504"],"rows":[["Freq. (f)","1","2","4","2","1"]],"version":1}}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the population mean μ and population standard deviation σ (to 2 d.p.). [2]\n(b) Batch approval requires "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"499.0 \\le \\mu \\le 501.0"}},{"type":"text","text":" mL and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\sigma \\le 2.00"}},{"type":"text","text":" mL. Justify whether the batch passes. [1]"}]}]},"e":"(a) $\\mu=5000/10=500.0$ mL; $\\Sigma f(x-500)^2=48$, so $\\sigma=\\sqrt{4.8}\\approx2.19$ mL. (b) The mean is within limits, but σ = 2.19 mL > 2.00 mL, so the batch fails inspection.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"Water-sachet quality control","st":"population mean and σ against limits","cfg":{"question_number":42,"paper_question_key":42,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Mean and σ"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"Approval decision"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table"],"paper_title":"Water-sachet quality control","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":43,"t":"open_ended","p":"Athletics trial selection\nCumulative frequencies for 80 sprinters'' 100 m times $t$ (s):\n\n[Figure: table]\n(a) State Q1 and Q3 and calculate the IQR of sprint times. [2]\n(b) Runners with $t \\le 12.0$ s make the squad; the fastest 15% overall earn grants. State squad size and justify whether $t = 12.0$ s guarantees a grant. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Athletics trial selection","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cumulative frequencies for 80 sprinters'' 100 m times "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"t"}},{"type":"text","text":" (s):"}]},{"type":"assessDiagramBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"kind":"table","title":"","columns":["t ≤ (s)","11.5","12.0","12.5","13.0","13.5","14.0","14.5"],"rows":[["Cum. freq.","0","8","20","44","60","74","80"]],"version":1}}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State Q1 and Q3 and calculate the IQR of sprint times. [2]\n(b) Runners with "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"t \\le 12.0"}},{"type":"text","text":" s make the squad; the fastest 15% overall earn grants. State squad size and justify whether "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"t = 12.0"}},{"type":"text","text":" s guarantees a grant. [1]"}]}]},"e":"(a) N = 80: Q1 is at CF 20 → 12.5 s; Q3 at CF 60 → 13.5 s; IQR = 1.0 s. (b) CF 8 are at or below 12.0 s, so the squad has 8 runners. The fastest 15% is 0.15 × 80 = 12 runners, and all 8 squad members rank inside the fastest 12, so 12.0 s guarantees a grant.","m":3,"df":"medium","tp":"Athletics trial selection","st":"quartiles and percentiles in context","cfg":{"question_number":43,"paper_question_key":43,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Quartiles and IQR"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"Squad size and grant decision"}],"response_guidance":"In sprinting smaller times are faster; the top 15% is the lowest 12 times.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","graph"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","graph"],"paper_title":"Athletics trial selection","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":44,"t":"open_ended","p":"Courier delivery times & outliers\nNine Porto-Novo courier trips take (min): 18, 19, 21, 22, 22, 23, 25, 26, 76 (the 76 min trip had a flat tyre).\n(a) Calculate the mean and median for all 9 trips, and again for the 8 normal trips. [2]\n(b) Advise which average best represents typical delivery time and why 76 min should stay in audit logs. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Courier delivery times & outliers","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Nine Porto-Novo courier trips take (min): 18, 19, 21, 22, 22, 23, 25, 26, 76 (the 76 min trip had a flat tyre)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the mean and median for all 9 trips, and again for the 8 normal trips. [2]\n(b) Advise which average best represents typical delivery time and why 76 min should stay in audit logs. [1]"}]}]},"e":"(a) All 9: Σx = 252, mean 28 min, median 22 min. Normal 8: Σx = 176, mean 22 min, median 22 min. (b) Advertise the median 22 min: the 9-trip mean 28 min is pulled up by the flat-tyre outlier, but 76 min is a genuine operational event and belongs in audit logs for reliability analysis.","m":3,"df":"medium","tp":"Courier delivery times & outliers","st":"mean vs median and audit retention","cfg":{"question_number":44,"paper_question_key":44,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Both sets of averages"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"Advice and audit rationale"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math"],"paper_title":"Courier delivery times & outliers","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}},{"n":45,"t":"open_ended","p":"Maize hybrid selection\nTwo maize hybrids tested across 16 plots each (yield in t/ha):\nAlpha: μ = 6.4, Med = 6.5, IQR = 0.7, σ = 0.45  |  Beta: μ = 6.9, Med = 6.8, IQR = 2.1, σ = 1.40.\n(a) Compare average yield and yield consistency using both central and dispersion measures. [2]\n(b) Recommend one hybrid for a cooperative that must avoid yields below 5.5 t/ha, justifying statistically. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Maize hybrid selection","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two maize hybrids tested across 16 plots each (yield in t/ha):"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Alpha: μ = 6.4, Med = 6.5, IQR = 0.7, σ = 0.45  |  Beta: μ = 6.9, Med = 6.8, IQR = 2.1, σ = 1.40."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Compare average yield and yield consistency using both central and dispersion measures. [2]\n(b) Recommend one hybrid for a cooperative that must avoid yields below 5.5 t/ha, justifying statistically. [1]"}]}]},"e":"(a) Beta has the higher mean (6.9 vs 6.4) and median (6.8 vs 6.5); Alpha is much more consistent (IQR 0.7 vs 2.1, σ 0.45 vs 1.40). (b) Choose Alpha: 5.5 t/ha is 2σ below its mean (6.4 − 2 × 0.45 = 5.50), whereas for Beta it is only 1σ below (6.9 − 1.40 = 5.50), so Beta carries a much higher risk of low yields.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"Maize hybrid selection","st":"comparing centres and spreads for a risk decision","cfg":{"question_number":45,"paper_question_key":45,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Average and consistency comparison"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"Risk-aware recommendation"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math"],"paper_title":"Maize hybrid selection","source":"myp4-term1-summative-first-term-2026-version-d"}}]'::jsonb) as x
